I was actually doing incorrect data testing in the previous weeks with logistic regression

Old approach:
  -> z-score (uses all the data stats)
    -> split and train data
    -> train model

New approach:
  Training data (80% 4-folds)
      -> Scalar.fit()
          ->(learns mean = 50, std = 10)
      -> Scalar.transform()
      -> LogisticRegression.fit()

  Test data(20%, 1 fold)
      ->Scaler.transform()
        ->(uses mean=50, std = 10)
      ->LogisticRegression.predict()
      ->then gives accuracy

How data is being tested and split:
  When calculating the mean before hand I used the entire dataset including test data which the model wouldve used to
  learn about the test set distribution

  pipline.fit(X_trian) only computes for each feautre 
  standardScalar transforms X_train using those statistics
  logisticRegression trains on the transformed training data

  The test data never influences the scaler parameters
  Original data:
    150 trials
    split into k=5 folds
    fold 1-5: 30 trials

    round1: train on folds 1,2,3,4 test on 5
    round2: train on folds 1,2,3,5 test on 4
    ...etc.

    average the 5 test accuracies = final accuracy
    used stratified so each fold has the same proportion of classes
    shuffled=True makes sure that its shuffles
    each fold gets its own scaler fitted on its own training data

